window.docContents[177].resolve({"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.SignalSession.Run/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___SignalSession___Run":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession › Run.leanTier 1 · statement file\n\nTier 1 · statement file used by the symbolic proof 3 headline theorems depend on it 253 lines trust banner B1, trusted: the statement source ↗Read in full. A statement file: its definitions say what the headline theorems claim (games, adversaries, bounds). Proofs are cut after := and shown as ⋯; Lean checks them.\n\nDeclarations on this page (33)structure Inputsdef stdioPathsdef fsViewdef filesOfinductive Outcomeabbrev CliRunstructure Addressdef Address.textinductive Cmddef Cmd.argvdef addressFiledef identityKeyPairFiledef registrationIdFiledef coinsFiledef identityFiledef sessionFiledef signedPreKeyFiledef oneTimePreKeyFiledef oneTimeKyberPreKeyFiledef lastResortKyberPreKeyFiledef baseKeysFiledef partyDirsdef le32structure PublicBundledef PublicBundle.filesdef bundleDirdef msgTypeWhisperdef msgTypePreKeydef observedef stdinCapBytesdef fileCapBytesabbrev cliPathMaxBytesdef maxPlaintextBytes\n\n\n\nThe signal-session CLI: commands, runs, outcomes, and directory layoutTrusted: the statement. Shared CLI run interface (Inputs, Outcome, CliRun), canonical file-system projections (fsView, filesOf), command-line syntax (Address, Cmd), party and bundle directory layout (partyDirs, PublicBundle), wire type bytes, and size caps (see Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md).Each CLI invocation runs in one party's directory with relative paths, reading argv, stdin, coins, and (for init) a bundle directory, and exiting with 0 (accepted, stores and stdout written), 1 (protocol rejection, files unchanged and stdout empty), 2 (usage or I/O error), or Outcome.aborted (panic = \"abort\"). This module imports only Arm64.Trusted.Process.Loader so both computational and symbolic statements can depend on it.\n\n\n\nRuns\n\nstructure InputsL37–55 ↗#The inputs of one run. Paths are relative to the party's directory, the process's working directory, as the CLI opens them. The engine looks a path up by its canonical form (Arm64.normalizePath drops empty and . segments, so a, ./a and a// name one file), and its loader refuses a path with a leading / or a .. segment.* argv: the arguments after argv[0] (the program name, \"signal-session\");* dirs: the directories that exist (the CLI never creates one);* files: the regular files that exist, (path, bytes), entered in order: a later entry for a path wins, and a later entry below a path makes that path a directory;* stdin: the bytes on file descriptor 0.\n\ndef stdioPathsL57–61 ↗#The names of the standard streams in the engine's file system, which are never files of the party's directory. Some engine versions' loaders (Arm64.Process.initFsState) write stdin's bytes at \"<stdin>\" and empty files at \"<stdout>\" and \"<stderr>\"; others keep the streams out of the file system. fsView hides these names either way.\n\ndef fsViewL63–73 ↗#The regular files of an engine file system, as a lookup: the bytes at each path that holds a regular file (FsState.files, by the path's canonical form); none at a directory, at a missing path, and at the standard streams' names (stdioPaths, compared in canonical form). The model side (observe, through filesOf) and the binary side (toOutcome, CliRun.lean) both read files through it.\n\ndef filesOfL75–82 ↗#The files of a list of (path, bytes) entries, as a lookup: fsView of the file system the engine's loader builds from them (Arm64.Process.initFsState, with no directories and an empty stdin). So a later entry for a path wins (paths compared in canonical form), a path that a later entry makes a directory is none, and so are the stdio names. Directories are none in fsView, so the directories a run starts with do not change this lookup. A missing file and an empty file differ here; the CLI reads both as empty (stores.rs, read_file).\n\ninductive OutcomeL84–94 ↗#The outcome of one run:* exited code stdout files: the process exited with code, having written stdout, and left the regular files files (a lookup by path; none at missing paths, directories and the stdio paths);* aborted: libsignal panicked (panic = \"abort\"). The exit code is a Nat. The engine's process result carries it as a machine word (the exit_group argument): the bridge from the engine (SignalSession/Process.lean, outcomeOf) maps it with .toNat. The specs use the codes 0, 1 and 2.\n\nabbrev CliRunL96–99 ↗#Any implementation of the CLI: the outcome of the run on Inputs, none if there is no outcome (the run faults, gets stuck, or never ends). The statements are proved for every run : CliRun that meets a domain spec.\n\nThe command line\n\nstructure AddressL103–109 ↗#A protocol address <service id>.<device id> (lib.rs, address): a service id string and a device id. The specs use the two parties' fixed addresses (SmDir.lean).\n\ndef Address.textL111–113 ↗#The address as text: name ++ \".\" ++ device (ProtocolAddress's Display). This is the argument the CLI parses and the name of its session file.\n\ninductive CmdL115–121 ↗#The commands the specs cover (lib.rs, Call): init <peer> <bundle dir> starts a session with peer from the bundle directory; send <peer> encrypts stdin to peer; recv <peer> decrypts stdin from peer.\n\ndef Cmd.argvL123–127 ↗#The arguments of a command (after argv[0]).\n\nThe party's directory (stores.rs, public.rs)\n\ndef addressFileL131–132 ↗#The party's own address, as text.\n\ndef identityKeyPairFileL133–134 ↗#The party's IdentityKeyPair.\n\ndef registrationIdFileL135–136 ↗#The party's registration id, u32 little-endian.\n\ndef coinsFileL137–138 ↗#The randomness of the next call, written by the caller.\n\ndef identityFileL139–140 ↗#The pinned identity key of the account name (one per account).\n\ndef sessionFileL141–142 ↗#The SessionRecord with the device a.\n\ndef signedPreKeyFileL143–144 ↗#A SignedPreKeyRecord, by id (decimal).\n\ndef oneTimePreKeyFileL145–146 ↗#A one-time PreKeyRecord, by id; emptied when used.\n\ndef oneTimeKyberPreKeyFileL147–149 ↗#A one-time KyberPreKeyRecord, by id; emptied when used.\n\ndef lastResortKyberPreKeyFileL150–152 ↗#A last-resort KyberPreKeyRecord, by id; kept.\n\ndef baseKeysFileL153–156 ↗#The handshakes a last-resort Kyber pre-key has accepted: (signed pre-key id:u32 ‖ base key[33])*.\n\ndef partyDirsL158–164 ↗#The directories of a party that the caller creates (the CLI never creates a directory): the six private ones and public/ with its four. Root-level files (address, coins, …) need no directory.\n\nBundle directories (public.rs)\n\ndef le32L168–170 ↗#The 4 little-endian bytes of a u32 (u32::to_le_bytes).\n\nstructure PublicBundleL172–194 ↗#What a bundle directory holds, as the bytes the peer published (public.rs): its identity key (serialized), its registration id, a signed pre-key (id, serialized public key, signature), a Kyber pre-key (id, serialized public key, signature) and possibly a one-time pre-key (id, serialized public key).\n\ndef PublicBundle.filesL196–207 ↗#The files of bundle directory dir (public.rs, read_bundle): identity-key, registration-id, signed-pre-key and kyber-pre-key (each id:u32 ‖ key ‖ signature), and one-time-pre-key (id:u32 ‖ key) if the bundle has one. A missing one-time-pre-key reads as empty, i.e. none.\n\ndef bundleDirL209–210 ↗#The bundle directory the specs pass to init.\n\nMessages on stdin and stdout (lib.rs, run / decode_wire)\n\ndef msgTypeWhisperL214–216 ↗#The first byte of a message send prints and recv reads for a bare SignalMessage (CiphertextMessageType::Whisper).\n\ndef msgTypePreKeyL218–220 ↗#The first byte for a PreKeySignalMessage (CiphertextMessageType::PreKey). Any other first byte, and an empty message, are rejected by recv.\n\nObserving a model's outcome\n\ndef observeL224–235 ↗#The outcome a run must have when the model of the call says r, for a call on the files files (none = libsignal panics; some none = libsignal rejects; some (some (writes, out)) = libsignal accepts, the stores write writes, and stdout is out):* a panic is aborted;* a rejection exits 1 with an empty stdout and every file as it was;* an acceptance exits 0 with stdout out and the files files overwritten by writes.\n\nInput and file-size caps\n\ndef stdinCapBytesL239–241 ↗#Maximum bytes signal_session_io::read_stdin accepts (1 MiB = 2^20 = 1,048,576). Reused across both send (before the 10^6-byte plaintext check) and recv.\n\ndef fileCapBytesL243–244 ↗#Maximum bytes signal_session_io::read_file accepts (2 MiB = 2^21 = 2,097,152).\n\nabbrev cliPathMaxBytesL246–248 ↗#PATH_MAX = 4096: paths of 4096 bytes or more, or with an interior NUL, are rejected (longest accepted: 4095).\n\ndef maxPlaintextBytesL250–251 ↗#Maximum plaintext byte length accepted by send (10^6 = 1,000,000).\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession/Run.lean","id":"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.SignalSession.Run/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___SignalSession___Run"}});